// Dates, clock values and the passage of time: the `date` or `time` a value
// falls on, the `duration` between two of them, and the `timer` that fires
// every duration.
//
// None of this is ambient. A program that never asks what time it is never
// names any of it, which is why these are imported rather than in scope
// everywhere.
//
// `date`, `time` and `datetime` are string-representable, as `color` is: a
// string literal in the canonical form coerces to one, and one coerces back
// to that form. Platform codegen maps all three to the host's native
// date/time type (Go `time.Time`, JS/Kotlin an RFC3339 string).
//
// `now` is the clock, `datetime.add` moves an instant by a duration, and
// `between` reports the duration from one instant to another -- with `since`
// and `until` the two halves of that with the current instant supplied. A
// duration divided by a duration is the dimensionless number of times one
// goes into the other; divided by a plain number it is still a duration.
//
// This is **wall-clock** time, which is what a date shown to someone is and
// what an interval measured against a calendar deadline is. It is not a
// scheduling primitive: a wall clock jumps on an NTP correction, on a daylight
// saving change and on a manual adjustment, so anything that has to measure
// *elapsed* time reliably wants a monotonic reading, which none of these is.
